it's beginning of October which means  the Supreme Court is back in session  after it's summer recess we're you know  we're looking at another very exciting  year where the court tackles some of the  most politically divisive issues in  American life and here are three cases  to watch this term really first big  controversial case of this term is is  case out of Texas dealing with the use  of racial preferences or affirmative  action in higher education that's Fisher  reverse University of Texas at Austin  does equal protection under the law of  mean that you treat everyone the same  that essentially the Constitution is  colorblind and that's how equal  protections guaranteed and then on the  other side says that equal protection  requires equality of opportunity and  that requires the government or the  state university in this case to craft  race-conscious measures that take race  into account in order to create a  diversity on campus what Texas is  calling a critical mass of  underrepresented students the second  case worth watching this term is an  Arkansas Game and Fish Commission versus  United States so at issue here is that  the US Army Corps of Engineers as part  of its flood management and maintaining  this dam in southern Missouri flooded  property 23,000 acres of property owned  by this state agency in Arkansas and  caused five-and-a-half million dollars  worth of damage it's largely due to  trees so this has to do with the takings  Clause of the Fifth Amendment which says  that when the government takes a private  property for public use it must pay just  compensation and so this is a you know  subsection of takings clause but it's  quite important and certainly for any  homeowners property owners business  owners whose land could lie within what  could potentially be a floodplain thanks  to the US Army Corps of Engineers the  outcome of this case matters the third  case to keep an eye on is actually it's  actually two cases being heard on the  same day out of Florida dealing with the  use of drug sniffing dogs by law  enforcement that the police used a drug  dog in one case to sniff outside of a  home and then the dog gave its alert and  said you know there's marijuana growing  inside the home and this was done  without a warrant and did the and so the  question is is did police need a warrant  do they need to show probable cause in  order to bring a drug dog to somebody's  house the other case is we having do  with the drugs dog searching a car is  especially interesting  the florida supreme court in that case  got into the issue of how is this dog  trained you know what what assurances do  the courts have these dogs are properly  trained do we know how they behaved in  other situations do they always get the  same allure they do something different  and also the handler is the handler  properly trained and certified how do we  know this what sort of assurances do the  courts have that this dog is not just  doing what the handlers want to do as a  way of getting around a probable cause  requirement so it's going to be very  interesting to see what the Supreme  Court has to say about that because that  that's that would particularly limit law  enforcement use of these sort of animals  in drug cases
 

